          Blur in digital images typically stems from three primary factors:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-[#171717]">Optical Defocus Blur:</strong> Occurs when the camera lens focal plane misses the intended subject. Depth-of-field constraints cause the background or foreground to be in focus while your subject remains soft.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-[#171717]">Motion & Camera Shake:</strong> Caused by movement during the exposure window. Slower shutter speeds in low-light environments cause hand tremors or subject motion to create directional streaks across image sensor pixels.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-[#171717]">Digital Compression & Downsampling:</strong> Sending photos through messaging apps or social networks aggressively compresses resolution, stripping high-frequency edge information and creating fuzzy compression artifacts.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
